Goals, Objectives and Learning Outcomes of the Course
Course Description | Applied Mathematical Models - basic course at Astana IT University; it plays an important role in understanding science, technology, economics, and computer science, as well as other disciplines. This course, Applied Mathematical Models, covers algorithms and methods for solving the Cauchy problem, boundary value problems, and other problems posed for differential equations. Course Goal(s) | Course Objective
The objective of the course is that upon completion of the course, students will be able to recognize and master the concepts of applied mathematical models from a user's perspective. Course Objectives:
● Introduce the basic ideas of applied mathematical models, including applications of various boundary value problems of ordinary differential equations and problems of partial differential equations. ● Apply the basic concepts of applied mathematical models to various applications. Course Learning Outcomes Students who successfully complete the course will be able to:
● Model real-world processes described by ordinary differential equations. ● Model and create algorithms and codes for solving real-world processes described by partial differential equations; Course Objectives: | Students should master the following concepts:
• model Cauchy problems for linear ordinary differential equations of the first order.
- model Cauchy problems for linear ordinary differential equations of the second order with constant coefficients. • model Cauchy problems for a system of linear ordinary differential equations of the second order with constant coefficients.
- model Dirichlet problems for linear ordinary differential equations of the second order with constant coefficients;
- model the first mixed boundary value problem for linear ordinary differential equations of the second order with constant coefficients;
- model the second mixed boundary value problem for linear ordinary differential equations of the second order with constant coefficients;
- model the third mixed boundary value problem for linear ordinary differential equations of the second order with constant coefficients;
- model a mixed boundary value problem for an equation of hyperbolic type;
- model a mixed boundary value problem for an equation of parabolic type;
- model a mixed boundary value problem for an elliptic equation; Skills & Competences | Upon completion of this course, students should demonstrate competence in the following skills:
• model processes described by Cauchy problems of first-order ordinary differential equations;
- model processes described by Cauchy problems of second-order linear ordinary differential equations with constant coefficients;
- model processes described by the Dirichlet problem of second-order linear ordinary differential equations with constant coefficients;
- model processes described by the Dirichlet and Neumann problem of second-order linear ordinary differential equations with constant coefficients;
- model processes described by the Dirichlet and Robin problem of second-order linear ordinary differential equations with constant coefficients; • model processes described by hyperbolic equations;
- model processes described by parabolic equations;
- model processes described by elliptic equations; Course Learning Outcomes: | By the end of this course, students will be able to understand the concepts involved in modeling Cauchy and boundary value problems, as well as modeling processes that describe various equations of mathematical physics.

Course Objectives: | Course objectives include:
To understand basics of traditional computer vision and data driven computer vision; To understand basics of machine learning; To understand the definition of a range of neural network models; To understand optimization algorithms for neural network models; To understand and implement various neural network algorithms in a range of real-world vision applications. Skills & Competences | Programming. Analytical. Problem solving. Critical thinking. Communication. Course Learning Outcomes: | Upon completion of the course the student should have the following learning outcomes defined in terms of knowledge, skills and general competence:
explain the basic principles behind neural networks and deep learning;
compare modeling aspects of various neural network architectures;
implement simple neural network algorithms;
apply and evaluate deep learning on real data sets;
provide successful examples how deep learning can be used in different contexts in the society;
read and critically assess papers on artificial neural networks and their applications. Methods of Assessment | The expected learning outcomes for the course will be assessed through graded activities and ungraded activities. The graded activities include assignments and exams. The ungraded activities will be used to monitor progress of students. Assignment 1
